Slythe <laurens.nospam@winkelhagen.com> deserves a cookie for saying:
> I hope one of you can help me with this.

> I installed nethack in my brand new debian (woody, 3.0) configuration,
> but it doesn't work I get the following error message:
> /lib/libc.so.6: version `GLIBC_3.2' not found (required by
> /usr/games/lib/nethackdir/nethack)

> I already have the newest libc6 (2.5, apparently!) so I'm at quite a
> loss here.

Woody is Stable and thus fairly old.  The best you can do for glibc is
2.5, I think.  Testing (Sarge) gives you 3.2-7 and unstable (Sid) 3.2-8.

> Meanwhile, the 3.4.0 version works just fine. Maybe thats not so odd,
> since that one came as a .deb package form debian.

Yep.  Stable is only up to 3.4.0 and testing/unstable are at 3.4.1.

> Is there something I can do, or should I just wait for a newer Debian
> version?

You can change your distro to Testing or Unstable, if you're in the
mood.  ;)  That'll give you the proper glibc to install the binary.  

Or, and this is what I recommend, you compile your own.  Just grab the
source instead of the binary and go to it.  

